WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.230
Like any job, acting can be tough if you fail
to prepare correctly.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:09.200
But unlike a lot of jobs, what an actor is
expected to do on any given day can vary dramatically

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.469
depending on the project they're working on.

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:15.519
As a result, there are countless stories of
actors being hilariously underprepared for

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:20.760
roles due to time constraints, overconfidence,
and sometimes, just plain old bad luck.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.400
Benedict Cumberbatch and the penguin doc

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.090
Benedict Cumberbatch has played everything
from an American neurosurgeon-wizard to Sherlock

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.090
Holmes.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.500
And with such an impressive résumé, you'd
think voiceover would be a cinch for the actor.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.850
But Cumberbatch has a very peculiar acting
Achilles' heel: he can;t say "penguin".

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.370
"So why are these woodlands so attractive
to pengwings?"

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.400
Cumberbatch apparently wasn't aware of this,
and in 2009, took a voiceover gig for the

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.110
BBC documentary South Pacific, in which he
struggled to pronounce the word almost every

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:51.110
single time.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:56.899
"These are royal pengwings and they're about
to have their first sighting of land in seven

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:57.899
months"

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:01.719
On The Graham Norton Show, Cumberbatch admitted
his amazement that nobody working on the doc

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:02.719
corrected him.

00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:06.470
"It's not just me sitting in a booth going
I don't think I know how to say penguin, I'll

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:07.680
say pengwings.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.110
It's mortifying"

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.400
Peter Weller and the RoboCop suit

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.750
The titular RoboCop was played by Peter Weller,
who was hired to portray the character due

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.850
to his slight build and his strong jawline.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.050
"(target shooting) This guy is really good.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.340
He's not a guy.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:23.900
He's a machine.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:24.900
(blade sound)"

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.740
Weller had planned to spend several weeks
getting used to walking around in the RoboCop

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.430
suit to make his movements seem natural.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.390
However, due to the suit's last-minute tweaks,
it wasn't ready ahead of time.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.290
Weller was instead forced to begin shooting
the day same it arrived on set.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:43.281
"The suit was ready when it was ready"
"The second that we cut, immediately, like

00:01:43.281 --> 00:01:46.650
a team of Ferrari mechanics, these guys would
go to work on me"

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:51.160
This was so frustrating for Weller that production
had to be stopped for three days so he could

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.460
work with the suit.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.690
The rigid, robotic movements audiences would
soon associate with RoboCop were really a

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.490
byproduct of Weller learning to move without
fighting against the suit.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.900
Hugh Jackman's doughy Wolverine

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.440
To a lot of people, Hugh Jackman is Wolverine.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.880
"Excuse me I'm Eric Lensherr and I'm Xavier
Go f--- yourself"

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.780
The actor has put an astonishing amount of
effort into physically portraying the character

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.230
— something which is especially notable
in later movies in the X-Men series, where

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:22.500
Jackman appears way more ripped than he was
in the first film in the franchise.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.330
Jackman has since said that he was embarrassed
by his physique in the first X-Men movie,

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:27.330
telling Stephen Colbert,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.842
"I thought you could get into shape in 3 weeks…
and I turned up and the director had to push

00:02:31.842 --> 00:02:35.570
all the shirt off scenes to the very end four
months later"

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:39.320
Director Bryan Singer may have pushed those
shirtless scenes as long as he could, but

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.420
Jackman says he's still embarrassed about
them.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.510
Kit Harrington is buff, not a history buff

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.260
During the press tour for Pompeii, a film
about what happened to Kit Harrington's abs

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.150
during a volcanic eruption, Harrington admitted
that he hadn't done any research into the

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:56.050
actual destruction of the Roman city Pompeii
in the year 79 A.D.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.910
Instead, he told the BBC he just figured Hollywood
always gets these things right.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:04.010
"I'm ashamed to say I didn't do a whole amount
of historical research, I took the script

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:05.010
as gospel.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.310
[...] But I went after we wrapped.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.880
[...] I was pleasantly surprised to see that
we were very historically accurate.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:14.240
I mean, we're a big-budget action movie and
we're bound to take historical liberties.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.240
But not many."

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.440
"We were very painstaking in re-creating Pompeii
in Toronto."

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.120
Ewan McGregor and the lightsaber noises

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:26.680
Like millions of people, Ewan McGregor is
a big fan of the Star Wars franchise, and

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.319
when he was cast as Obi-Wan Kenobi in the
prequels, he had trouble setting aside his

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:32.020
childlike glee around his lightsaber.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.840
"To begin with, it was quite difficult not
to make the sound...zuuh.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:40.840
And also when it goes away it makes that nice
ssssssuk noise when it goes down"

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.290
The actor would later admit that he messed
up quite a few takes because he couldn't help

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.470
making lightsaber noises whenever he swung
his prop around.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:51.550
According to a persistent rumor, a sound guy
finally had to tell McGregor that they add

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.400
sound effects in post, when they noticed his
humming noises were being picked up by the

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.959
boom mic.

00:03:56.959 --> 00:03:59.880
Marlon Brando's clueless, tubby warrior

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.770
Marlon Brando's not-giving-a-crap during production
was the stuff of legend.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:08.150
Notoriously indifferent when it came to reading
scripts and learning lines, Brando often turned

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.800
up on set completely unprepared.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.569
"I think you've summed it up admirably"

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:16.840
Take Apocalypse Now, where Brando was so clueless
about his role that production had to shut

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.310
down for an entire week so director Francis
Ford Coppola could read the script to him

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.870
and explain his character.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.310
Producer Fred Roos remembered,

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:31.440
"The whole company was sitting up on the set
all poised to shoot and Francis and Marlon

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:32.970
would be talking about the character."

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.440
On top of this, the ending to the film had
to be entirely rewritten because Brando, who

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.540
was playing "a lean and hungry warrior," weighed
roughly 300 pounds.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:46.270
To get around this, Coppola shot Brando almost
exclusively in shadow and made him wear all

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:50.770
black—partly because there wasn't a Green
Beret uniform to fit him.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:53.400
Finn Jones' last-minute choreography

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.880
Fans and critics alike took issue with the
subpar fight choreography of Marvel's Iron

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.410
Fist and its reliance on excessive cuts to
hide actor Finn Jones' apparent lack of martial

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:07.590
arts skill—a fairly major problem, considering
Iron Fist is supposed to be a master of all

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.020
forms of unarmed combat.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:14.130
"When she sees how good of a fighter he is,
that's when she starts to realize there's

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.199
something else about him"

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:19.800
Jones later explained that he literally had
minutes to learn the choreography for his

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.850
fight scenes, revealing to The Telegraph,

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:25.690
“I was learning those fight scenes just
15 minutes before we shot them."

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:28.980
That, and he had only three weeks to train
before filming started.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:33.669
"But I'm Danny Rand and I am the Iron Fist"

00:05:33.669 --> 00:05:36.210
James McAvoy and the haircut

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.810
When he learned he'd be playing Professor
X in X-Men: First Class, James McAvoy naturally

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:45.490
assumed that since he was playing the bald-headed
superhero previously played by Patrick Stewart,

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.800
the studio would want him to be similarly
shorn.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.919
"This is very important to me and if I can
help you I will do my utmost"

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:57.389
He shaved off all his hair before filming
started—but apparently the studio wanted

00:05:57.389 --> 00:06:01.400
McAvoy to have hair for the role since it
was set in the '60s, forcing the actor to

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.180
ask a hairstylist friend to add on hair extensions.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:09.650
A wig was apparently also used for some scenes,
though it's not clear which ones—which probably

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:11.830
means it was a pretty good wig.

